<mapreducescript scriptid="customscript_mcgi_mr_trader_cache_arch">
  <description>Trader Screen cache builder for CWP ARCH (subsidiary 9). Builds On Hand from lot balances; the other five buckets are structurally zero until ARCH sales orders and purchase orders exist in the account.</description>
  <isinactive>F</isinactive>
  <name>MCGI MR Trader Screen Cache ARCH</name>
  <notifyadmins>F</notifyadmins>
  <notifyemails>aserban@houseblend.io</notifyemails>
  <!-- Notifications go to Andrei ONLY while ARCH is being built.
       notifyowner=T sent them to the script owner, "House Blend 2"
       (credentials.ns_mcg2@houseblend.io) — a shared HouseBlend
       credentials mailbox, so the person actually building this never
       saw them. notifyadmins stays F: MGSL admins must not be paged by
       our in-progress work. -->
  <notifyowner>F</notifyowner>
  <scriptfile>[/SuiteScripts/mcgi_services/trader_screen/entry_points/mr/mcgi_mr_trader_screen_cache_arch.js]</scriptfile>
  <!-- ONE script parameter, because the code actually reads it.
       The MTL fork carries four; the other three are still deliberately absent —
       the subsidiary is a constant, there is no delta mode to threshold, and no
       lot costing yet to point at an accounting book. Declaring parameters the
       code never reads is how a deployment ends up looking configurable when it
       is not. -->
  <scriptcustomfields>
    <scriptcustomfield scriptid="custscript_ts_arch_force_full_rebuild">
      <accesslevel>2</accesslevel>
      <applyformatting>F</applyformatting>
      <checkspelling>F</checkspelling>
      <defaultchecked>F</defaultchecked>
      <defaultselection></defaultselection>
      <defaultvalue></defaultvalue>
      <description>Bypasses the shrink guard for ONE run. Check this only when the summary is legitimately much smaller than what is cached — for example after test data is deleted. Leave unchecked: with it on, a broken run can silently truncate the cache, which is exactly what the guard prevents.</description>
      <displayheight></displayheight>
      <displaytype>NORMAL</displaytype>
      <displaywidth></displaywidth>
      <dynamicdefault></dynamicdefault>
      <fieldtype>CHECKBOX</fieldtype>
      <help>The shrink guard refuses to replace the cached summary when the incoming row count is under half the cached count, because that pattern means a partial or errored run rather than a real change. If the shrink is genuine the guard would otherwise block it forever, so check this, run once, then UNCHECK it.</help>
      <isformula>F</isformula>
      <ismandatory>F</ismandatory>
      <label>Force Full Rebuild (bypass shrink guard)</label>
      <linktext></linktext>
      <maxlength></maxlength>
      <maxvalue></maxvalue>
      <minvalue></minvalue>
      <onparentdelete></onparentdelete>
      <searchlevel>2</searchlevel>
      <selectrecordtype></selectrecordtype>
      <setting></setting>
      <storevalue>T</storevalue>
    </scriptcustomfield>
    <scriptcustomfield scriptid="custscript_ts_arch_cost_book">
      <accesslevel>2</accesslevel>
      <applyformatting>F</applyformatting>
      <checkspelling>F</checkspelling>
      <defaultchecked>F</defaultchecked>
      <defaultselection></defaultselection>
      <defaultvalue>1</defaultvalue>
      <description>Accounting book the AVG COST column is priced in. 1 = Primary, which on a CAD subsidiary IS the CAD cost — this is what the client asked for twice (Marc-Antoine and Lucas, both "comme IND, en CAD"). Change it only if that decision is reversed; the code needs no edit.</description>
      <displayheight></displayheight>
      <displaytype>NORMAL</displaytype>
      <displaywidth></displaywidth>
      <dynamicdefault></dynamicdefault>
      <fieldtype>INTEGER</fieldtype>
      <help>MTL prices its lots in a USD SECONDARY book, whose internal id differs per environment (sandbox and production disagree). The PRIMARY book is 1 in every environment, so leaving this at 1 is environment-independent. Note that a CAD cost against a USD sales order makes the margin compare two currencies — that conversion rule is still an open question with the client and this parameter does not answer it.</help>
      <isformula>F</isformula>
      <ismandatory>F</ismandatory>
      <label>Costing Accounting Book</label>
      <linktext></linktext>
      <maxlength></maxlength>
      <maxvalue></maxvalue>
      <minvalue></minvalue>
      <onparentdelete></onparentdelete>
      <searchlevel>2</searchlevel>
      <selectrecordtype></selectrecordtype>
      <setting></setting>
      <storevalue>T</storevalue>
    </scriptcustomfield>
  </scriptcustomfields>
  <scriptdeployments>
    <scriptdeployment scriptid="customdeploy_mcgi_mr_trader_cache_arch">
      <buffersize>1</buffersize>
      <concurrencylimit>1</concurrencylimit>
      <!-- Left OFF at the deployment level so the guard is armed by default.
           It is a one-run switch, not a setting. -->
      <custscript_ts_arch_force_full_rebuild>F</custscript_ts_arch_force_full_rebuild>
      <!-- Primary book = CAD on a CAD subsidiary. See the field help. -->
      <custscript_ts_arch_cost_book>1</custscript_ts_arch_cost_book>
      <isdeployed>T</isdeployed>
      <loglevel>DEBUG</loglevel>
      <queueallstagesatonce>T</queueallstagesatonce>
      <runasrole>ADMINISTRATOR</runasrole>
      <!-- SCHEDULED hourly, deliberately, 2026-08-18.
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           This looks like it contradicts the standing rule "never enable the
           trader-cache MR's scheduled deployment". It does not. That rule is
           about the IND builder, and it exists for three specific mechanisms,
           ALL THREE OF WHICH ARE ABSENT HERE — verified against both files on
           2026-08-18:

             1. IND throttles on TS_LAST_RUN_TIMESTAMP with a non-atomic
                read-then-write, so two runners both decide "not throttled" and
                both start a FULL rebuild. This builder has NO throttle state at
                all (IND: 13 references to LAST_RUN, ARCH: 0).
             2. IND's summarize READS TS_SUMMARY, merges, and writes back, so
                interleaved runs mean last-writer-wins on a partial merge and
                one run's delta silently vanishes. This summarize never reads
                SUMMARY back — it writes a complete payload built from the
                output iterator, so a concurrent run can only write the same
                bytes.
             3. IND's summarize self-reschedules onto
                runtime.getCurrentScript().deploymentId, so enabling a second
                deployment forks an immortal chain. This builder contains no
                N/task, no task.submit and no deploymentId reference (IND: 1 and
                3 respectively).

           So ARCH is a stateless, idempotent FULL rebuild on a SINGLE
           deployment with concurrencylimit 1. It was scheduled because the
           opposite failure was real and observed: TTL was 4h and nothing
           refreshed the cache, so the summary endpoint returned CACHE_MISS
           overnight and the screen had no data until someone pressed a button.

           TWO RULES IF YOU CHANGE THIS:
             - Never add a second deployment to this script. One chain, one
               deployment. That is what keeps mechanism 3 inapplicable.
             - Keep TTL_SUMMARY/TTL_DETAIL above the refresh interval with
               margin. Raised to 12h on 2026-08-19: this schedule does NOT give
               24h coverage — measured, it repeats hourly from the 11:00Z start
               for ~16h then stops until the next day, leaving a ~8h gap. At 4h
               the cache died about four hours every night. 12h outlives the gap
               with margin. Lowering the TTL to "match" the hourly interval is
               the exact mistake that caused the IND blackout.

           `repeat` is an ISO 8601 duration — PT1H. Not a word enum. Taken from
           what SDF actually wrote after the change was made in the UI; do not
           guess this value. `starttime` is UTC: 11:00Z is 07:00 PT. -->
      <status>SCHEDULED</status>
      <title>MCGI MR Trader Screen Cache ARCH</title>
      <yieldaftermins>60</yieldaftermins>
      <recurrence>
        <daily>
          <everyxdays>1</everyxdays>
          <repeat>PT1H</repeat>
          <startdate>2026-08-18</startdate>
          <starttime>11:00:00Z</starttime>
        </daily>
      </recurrence>
    </scriptdeployment>
  </scriptdeployments>
</mapreducescript>
